<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qavurt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1073">1073</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Kavurt, Qara Arslan Beg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qavurt was a Seljuk prince, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaghri-beg">Chaghri Beg</a> and elder brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a>, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> in the 1040s and founded the Seljuk dynasty there. He extended his rule to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> across the Gulf. After Alp Arslan's death he claimed the sultanate, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik Shah</a> defeated him near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> in 1073 and had him killed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qavurt.jpg" alt="Qavurt" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LouisAragon (uploader) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Ahmad_Qavurt,_minted_in_Jiroft.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Qavurt, in full Kara Arslan Ahmad Qavurt, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaghri-beg">Chaghri Beg</a>, the Seljuk governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, and a nephew of Sultan Toghrul. Toghrul divided the conquered lands among his relatives and charged each with taking neighbouring territory, and Qavurt chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quhistan">Kuhistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>. Leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a>, he defeated the Buyid army in Kerman and Makran around 1041. The Buyid governor Bahram b. Lashkaristan withdrew to Bardasir and surrendered it about 1048, when Qavurt founded his own state there as a subject of Toghrul. He then crushed the Kufs, a bandit tribe of southern Kerman, and the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> submitted to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> and Yakuti he helped defeat the rebel Ibrahim Yinal near Rey in 1059. In 1062 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fadluya">Fadluya</a>, emir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabankara">Shabankara</a>, and beat him again the next year, but Fadluya turned to Alp Arslan, who drove Qavurt out of Fars. After Alp Arslan became sultan in 1063, Qavurt demanded a share of their uncle's treasury. In 1067 he struck his brother's name from the Friday sermon and minted coins in his own; defeated, he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiroft">Jiroft</a> and was pardoned.</p><p>Around 1068-69 he rebelled again with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fadluya">Fadluya</a> and was besieged at Bardasir, losing part of his lands. To give restless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> an outlet he sent forces against Baluchi brigands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, and crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> in ships hired from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> to depose the Buyid governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> died in 1072, leaving the throne to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik Shah I</a> and to Qavurt Fars, a share of the treasury and the hand of his chief consort. Qavurt hurried back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, losing ships and men in the crossing, and claimed the throne. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> officers favoured him, so Malik Shah and his vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> added non-Turkic regiments to the army. On 16 May 1073 Malik Shah defeated Qavurt at Kerç kapı near Hamedan.</p><p>Qavurt was captured and, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>'s urging, executed, as were two of his sons. His other sons ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik Shah</a>, and according to the chronicler Afdal al-Din Kirmani his descendants held it for over a century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qavurt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Qavurt</b> (Qara Arslan), elder brother of Alp Arslan, took the province of Kerman in the 1040s as the Seljuk Empire expanded across Iran.</blockquote><a href="/state/seljuk-sultanate-of-kerman">Seljuk Sultanate of Kerman</a></li></ul></section>
